## Streaming Large Uploads in ASP.NET Core 8 Minimal API

`IFormFile` buffers the entire request body into memory—that's why you OOM on large files. Instead, read the raw `Request.Body` (or use `Request.ReadFormAsync` with limits) and stream directly to disk.

### Recommended approach: read the multipart body manually

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Features;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.WebUtilities;
using Microsoft.Net.Http.Headers;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Raise the max request body size (default is ~28MB)
builder.WebHost.ConfigureKestrel(options =>
{
    options.Limits.MaxRequestBodySize = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024; // 2 GB
});

var app = builder.Build();

app.MapPost("/upload", async (HttpContext context) =>
{
    // Disable request body buffering so the stream isn't held in memory
    context.Features.Get<IHttpBodyControlFeature>()!.AllowSynchronousIO = false;

    var contentType = context.Request.ContentType;
    if (contentType is null || !contentType.Contains("multipart/"))
        return Results.BadRequest("Expected a multipart request.");

    var boundary = HeaderUtilities.RemoveQuotes(
        MediaTypeHeaderValue.Parse(contentType).Boundary).Value;

    if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(boundary))
        return Results.BadRequest("Missing boundary.");

    var reader = new MultipartReader(boundary, context.Request.Body);
    var section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync();

    while (section is not null)
    {
        var hasContentDisposition = ContentDispositionHeaderValue
            .TryParse(section.ContentDisposition, out var disposition);

        if (hasContentDisposition && disposition!.IsFileDisposition())
        {
            // Sanitize file name
            var fileName = Path.GetRandomFileName() +
                Path.GetExtension(disposition.FileName.Value);
            var savePath = Path.Combine("/tmp/uploads", fileName);

            Directory.CreateDirectory("/tmp/uploads");

            // Stream section directly to disk — no full-file buffering
            await using var targetStream = File.Create(savePath);
            await section.Body.CopyToAsync(targetStream);

            return Results.Ok(new { file = fileName });
        }

        section = await reader.ReadNextSectionAsync();
    }

    return Results.BadRequest("No file found in request.");
})
.DisableAntiforgery();

app.Run();
```

### Why this works

| Concern | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| Memory | `MultipartReader` reads section-by-section in small internal buffers (~16 KB). |
| Size limit | `MaxRequestBodySize` raised to 2 GB at the Kestrel level. |
| `IFormFile` bypass | You never bind to `IFormFile`; the raw stream flows straight to `FileStream`. |
| Form value attacks | `DisableFormValueModelBinding` (via not binding a model) means no hidden buffering. |

### Key configuration notes

1. **If behind IIS/HTTP.sys**, also set `maxAllowedContentLength` in `web.config`:
   ```xml
   <requestFiltering>
     <requestLimits maxAllowedContentLength="2147483648" />
   </requestFiltering>
   ```

2. **If using reverse proxy (nginx)**, set `client_max_body_size 2G;`.

3. **Optional**: pass a `MultipartReaderOptions` to tune the internal buffer:
   ```csharp
   var reader = new MultipartReader(boundary, context.Request.Body)
   {
       BodyLengthLimit = 2L * 1024 * 1024 * 1024
   };
   ```

This pattern keeps memory usage flat regardless of upload size.
